## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of MVCC pitfalls like write skew and phantom reads in distributed transactions. It probes concrete schemas, isolation choices, and cross-shard coordination strategies (2PC vs advisory locks).

## Key Concepts
- Write skew and phantom reads under SERIALIZABLE
- SELECT FOR UPDATE vs advisory locks
- Two-phase commit vs distributed locking
- Per-customer data partitioning and contention patterns

## Code Example
```sql
-- Pseudo-PL/pgSQL illustrating the flow
BEGIN;
SELECT 1 FROM customer_locks WHERE customer_id = :cid FOR UPDATE;
SELECT total_outstanding INTO v_total FROM customer_totals WHERE customer_id = :cid;
IF v_total + :newLoan > :cap THEN
  ROLLBACK;
  -- return error
ELSE
  INSERT INTO loans (...columns...) VALUES (...values...);
  UPDATE customer_totals SET total_outstanding = v_total + :newLoan WHERE customer_id = :cid;
  COMMIT;
END IF;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for write skew in CI/CD pipelines?
- What if 2PC adds prohibitive latency; what alternatives would you consider?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-store writes with explicit TTLs and cache invalidation. It checks how writes propagate across Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, and DynamoDB, plus how to keep a hot cache coherent.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-store write paths with idempotence
- TTL-based bucketing in Cassandra
- Cache invalidation and refresh strategies in Redis
- Read path with locality and fallback

## Code Example
```javascript
async function writeMessage(chatId, userId, text, ts){
  await redis.hset(`chat:presence:${userId}`, 'online', 1);
  const msgId = generateId();
  await mongo.messages.insertOne({chat_id: chatId, msg_id: msgId, sender_id: userId, ts, text});
  await cassandra.execute("INSERT INTO messages_by_chat (chat_id, bucket, ts, msg_id) VALUES (?, bucket(?), ?, ?)", [chatId, ts, ts, msgId]);
  await dynamo.archive({chat_id: chatId, msg_id: msgId, ts, text});
  await redis.publish(`chat:updates:${chatId}`, JSON.stringify({msg_id: msgId}));
}
```

